  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nScattered showers are ongoing early this morning as a trough\naxis swings through. The showers are expected to end from\nnorthwest to southeast during the next few hours as the axis\npropagates away from the region. Clouds will clear  Surface \nhigh pressure over the Rockies and northern Plains will continue\nto build south today. Gusty northwest winds have developed \nacross western MN and will continue to spread southeast this \nmorning. Forecast soundings indicate the potential for some\ngusts to reach 45 mph, but low level lapse rates aren't dry \nadiabatic due to the lack of strong cold air advection. \nTherefore, such gusts will be more sporadic with 35-40 mph gusts\nmore common. Winds will gradually subside this afternoon and \nbecome light and variable this evening as high pressure builds \nto the central Plains. \n\nWith the ridge axis overhead, clear skies, and light winds in \nplace this evening, temperatures should drop quickly into the\n30s. Frost formation is likely by late evening area wide. During\nthe overnight hours, the high will shift to the southern Plains\nand return flow will increase, particularly across central and\nwestern MN. This should halt additional frost formation, and may\nkeep temperatures from reaching freezing in those areas.\nTherefore, kept the Freeze Watch going across portions of\ncentral and western MN where winds should increase to around 10\nkts late tonight. Farther south and east, winds should remain\ndecoupled for much of the night and the likelihood of\nsubfreezing temperatures is higher. Upgraded southern MN and\nwestern WI to a Freeze Warning. The urban heat island of the Twin\nCities metro should keep temperatures above 32, but light winds\nand high RH with temps in the mid 30s should allow frost.\nUpgraded to a Frost Advisory there.\n\nWith southwest winds increasing late tonight, temperatures are\nexpected to make a rebound as early as tomorrow. Highs in the\nupper 50s to mid 60s are expected. Breezy conditions with wind\ngusts of 25 to 30 mph and RH in the 30s will lead to elevated\nfire weather conditions, especially in areas that largely missed\nthe soaking rain Thursday. A near repeat for Sunday, although\ntemperatures will warm to the mid to upper 60s.\n\nNext week will feature a ridge across much of the CONUS with\ntroughs on the east and west coasts. Very quiet and mild weather\nwill result and temperatures should reach the low to mid 70s \neach day from Tuesday onward, or 15 to 20 degrees above normal.\nA weak short wave passing through Wednesday will bring a low\nchance for rain, with additional chances coming next weekend as\nthe western long wave trough finally begins to approach.",
